  "query_preview": "..\n\nSoutheast Michigan still firmly entrenched within the western flank \nof a broad low pressure system ejecting northeast into New York. \nSustained upward vertical motion maintained under persistent mid \nlevel deformation will continue to capitalize on resident deep \nmoisture layer to generate intervals of light showers/drizzle into \nthe evening hours. As the governing system peels away and draws the \nmain deformation axis back across the area, there may be a noted \nbrief uptick in coverage/intensity before chances end from west to \neast overnight. Marginal depth to the freezing level with \ntemperatures holding in the mid 30s in locations will maintain \npotential for melting snow to mix in, particularly after sunset. \nMinimal drop to overnight temperature as weak shortwave ridging \narrives late. Lows generally in the vicinity of the freezing mark. \n\nLower amplitude mid level wave will pivot across the area Saturday. \nNarrow corridor of ascent will emerge along the lead edge of weak \nwarm air advection, but with model guidance largely holding this \nfeature north and west of the area. Outgoing forecast maintains a \nlow probability for light shower development mainly across the \nSaginaw valley Saturday afternoon. Otherwise, dry and seasonable \ndaylight conditions under a generous coverage of cloud cover. Highs \nin the mid 40s. \n\nAnother widespread precipitation event on target to arrive Sunday \nand Sunday night. A dynamic mid level wave of north pacific origin \nwill strengthen with time as it ejects out of the rockies, \neffectively establishing a deepening low pressure system that will \ntrack northeast across the Ohio valley. This positions southeast \nMichigan favorably to witness a meaningful period of ascent along \nthe north flank within a zone of deeper moisture transport. Once \nagain, assessment of sounding data and thickness levels point to \npredominately liquid as the precipitation type, with a peak in \nrainfall intensity centered during the afternoon and evening hours. \n24 hr qpf output from the grand ensemble points to a mean in \nrainfall total near half an inch, with an interquartile range from \n3\" to .7\" /highest south and east/. An increase in gustiness to \nnorth to northwest wind expected as the low tracks by just to the \neast during the evening. Peak gusts into the 30 mph range possible. \nThe cold rain ensures temperatures remain in the upper 30s to lower \n40s this period. \n\nUpper level pattern transitions toward a more zonal configuration \nduring the early-mid week period. A peak in cold air advection for \nthe Monday period in the wake of the exiting weekend system will \nbring the coolest conditions of the upcoming work week. Top of the \nexisting stratus deck may prove cold enough to provide a few passing \nflurries, but otherwise a lack of meaningful shortwave energy or \ngreater moisture quality this period will favor generally dry \nconditions through Wednesday. Prevailing low level flow from the \nsouth as upper level heights gradually build with time will offer a \nnotable warming trend, with temperatures pushing back into the 50s \nby the end of next week.",
